  "answer_text": "The universally accepted \"correct\" pronunciation of **doge** (as in the meme, the cryptocurrency Dogecoin, and the U.S. government acronym DOGE) is **\"dohj\"** (rhyming with *vogue* but ending in a soft \"j\" sound).\n\n### The Two Most Common (and Accepted) Pronunciations:\n1. **DOHJ** (ends with a standard \"j\" sound, like the \"g\" in *gently* or *giant*) \u2013 `/do\u028ad\u0292/`. This is the most common pronunciation in English dictionaries. \n2. **DOHZH** (ends with a softer \"zh\" sound, like the \"s\" in *measure*, *casual*, or *beige*) \u2013 `/do\u028a\u0292/`. This is also highly common and is often used by internet veterans.\n\n---\n\n### Why is it pronounced this way?\nThe pronunciation has two distinct origin stories that both point to the same \"dohj\" sound:\n\n* **The Web Series Origin (The Meme):** The spelling of \"doge\" as a misspelling of \"dog\" traces back to a June 2005 episode of the puppet web series ***Homestar Runner***. In the episode, the character Strong Bad spells out \"D-O-G-E\" while trying to distract Homestar and pronounces it **\"dohj\"**. The creators of Dogecoin officially cited this video as the reason they pronounce the cryptocurrency this way.\n* **The Historical Origin:** Long before the internet, \"doge\" was a real word. It refers to the elected chief magistrates (rulers) of historical Italian city-states like Venice and Genoa. In English, this historical title has always been pronounced **\"dohj\"**.\n\n---\n\n### Common Alternatives (and why they exist)\nBecause the word spread primarily through text on the internet, many people guessed the pronunciation before ever hearing it spoken aloud. You will frequently hear people say:\n* **\"Dohg\"** (rhyming with *vogue* or *rogue*, with a hard \"g\").\n* **\"Doggy\"** (pronouncing it like the actual animal).\n* **\"Dodge\"** (rhyming with *lodge* or *badge*, with a short \"o\").\n\nWhile you might hear these variations in casual conversation, if you want to pronounce it the way its creators, dictionary editors, and most tech/crypto spaces do, stick with **\"dohj\"**.",
